Spec: Plugin Updater Service

Purpose:
  Refresh managed plugins from their recorded Git repositories and requested versions.

Owns:
  ./plugin-updater.ts
  ./plugin-updater.test.ts

References:
  ../plugin-source/plugin-source.sdd
  ../plugin-registry/plugin-registry.sdd
  ../plugin-installer/plugin-installer.sdd
  ../logger/logger.sdd
  /src/cli-error.sdd

Must:
  Accept @PluginSource, @PluginRegistry, @PluginInstaller, and @Logger through constructor parameters.
  Accept an absolute project directory, an optional repository and plugin-name pair, and an optional version override.
  Read the current project's `.specdd/plugins.json` through @PluginRegistry.
  Select registered plugins whose resolved `src` lies beneath the project's `.specdd/plugins/` directory, retaining registry order.
  Skip registrations outside that directory without fetching or modifying their files, including when their names match a requested name.
  Select all managed registrations when neither repository nor plugin name is supplied.
  Require repository and plugin name together and require that pair for a version override; reject incomplete requests before reading the registry.
  Validate a supplied repository, plugin name, and version using @PluginSource.resolve before reading the registry.
  Match a targeted registration by the canonical managed path derived from repository coordinates and plugin name, so equivalent repository transports select the same installation.
  Allow identically named plugins from different repositories; update only the requested coordinate and report an uninstalled coordinate without falling back to another repository.
  Use the recorded origin for fetching even when an equivalent transport URL was supplied as a selector.
  Infer the plugin name from the parent directory of its registered `plugin.md` source path.
  Resolve a Git repository from `origin`, removing a recorded full commit fragment when present; preserve SSH transport and delegate authentication to the existing installer.
  Use the registration's version when no override is supplied; a recorded `latest` selects the remote default branch.
  Preserve an explicit override verbatim using add's version rules, including a literal Git ref named `latest`.
  Validate every selected repository, name, and version before starting any installation.
  Resolve `src` through @PluginRegistry so absolute, relative, and home-directory spelling can identify the same managed destination.
  Require every selected source to match the managed installation path derived from its recorded repository and inferred plugin name.
  Delegate replacement of each selected registration to @PluginInstaller.refresh so signature verification and confirmation, checksum checks, locking, atomic replacement, rollback, and registry preservation remain in force.
  Preserve original `src` spelling, entry order, additional metadata, and unselected registrations.
  Report an empty selection without a target as `No managed plugins installed.` without writing files or accessing Git.
  Process selected plugins sequentially; stop on the first failure, retain completed updates, and preserve the failed plugin according to installer rollback rules.
  Return each successful installation result in registry order and report the completed count through @Logger.

Exposes:
  @PluginUpdater
  @PluginUpdater.update
  @PluginUpdateRequest
  @PluginUpdateError

Scenario: retained versions
  Given one plugin records `latest` and another records `v1.2.0`
  When all plugins are updated
  Then the first follows the remote default branch and the second fetches `v1.2.0`

Scenario: repository-qualified override
  Given `review` is installed from both `\@acme/plugins` and `\@other/plugins`
  When `\@acme/plugins review` is updated to `v2.0.0`
  Then only the `acme/plugins` registration changes and its recorded version becomes `v2.0.0`
  And the `other/plugins` registration and plugin file are unchanged

Scenario: official repository update fails vendor verification
  Given a managed plugin records an origin under `github.com/specdd/`
  When its replacement lacks a valid signature from the bundled SpecDD keys
  Then the installer rejects the update without prompting and preserves the plugin and registry
